> Introducing your son to the world of Batman is a great choice, especially if you're looking for a classic series that offers sequels. A good starting point might be Tim Burton's "Batman" (1989), featuring Michael Keaton as Batman and Jack Nicholson as the Joker. It's a combination of stylish visuals and compelling storytelling that can engage both kids and adults.
> 
> This film is followed by "Batman Returns" (1992), which maintains the same gothic tone and continues the adventures of Keaton's Batman with new villains, including Catwoman and the Penguin.
> 
> Alternatively, if he's ready for a bit more complexity, Christopher Nolan's "The Dark Knight" trilogy is highly acclaimed. Starting with "Batman Begins" (2005), it offers a more modern and realistic take on Batman's origin story and builds in intensity with sequels "The Dark Knight" (2008) and "The Dark Knight Rises" (2012).
> 
> Ultimately, the choice depends on his age and how intense you think a Batman story should be. Tim Burton's films have a more comic book feel, while Nolan's trilogy explores deeper themes and is a bit darker. Either way, these films can provide a fantastic introduction to the Caped Crusader.
